The converse fails: $sum ((-1)^(n+1))/(n)$ converges by the alternating series test while $sum (1)/(n)$ diverges, so it converges without converging absolutely. Series that converge but not absolutely are called conditionally convergent.

Conditional convergence is a strictly weaker notion: such series can behave badly under rearrangements, whereas absolutely convergent series are insensitive to the order of summation.
